{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Last Light at Gannet Rock\n\nFor thirty-one years, Edwin Marrow climbed the iron stairs of Gannet Rock at dusk, wound the clockwork that turned the great lens, and lit the lamp that warned ships away from the reef. The island had no other residents. The nearest village was eleven miles east, across water that changed its mood every hour.\n\nHis wife, Ruth, had once told him that a lighthouse was a promise made to strangers. He had never quite understood the remark until the year the supply boat stopped coming.\n\nThe mainland office sent a letter in October saying the rock would be automated by spring. A solar panel and a battery would do the work of his hands. Edwin read the letter twice at the kitchen table, folded it neatly, and put it in the drawer where he kept the tide tables.\n\nHe did not tell anyone at first. He kept the lamp burning every night through the autumn gales, logging the wind speed and visibility in his careful square handwriting as he always had. The logbook had grown into eleven volumes, stacked on a shelf beside the cot. Sometimes he reread old entries: a fishing boat limping home in fog, a girl's birthday observed by a passing tanker that flashed its deck lights in salute, the night a storm had torn the railing away and he had lashed himself to the lantern room with his belt.\n\nIn December, a young inspector named Priya Anand came out on the last working supply run. She was thorough and kind, and she walked the tower with a clipboard, noting the brass fittings and the cracked glass of the south window.\n\n\"You've kept everything very well,\" she said at the top of the stairs, her breath clouding in the cold.\n\n\"I've kept it lit,\" Edwin said. \"That's different.\"\n\nShe looked at him for a long moment. \"The new system is reliable. It will never miss a night.\"\n\n\"I know. I read the specifications.\" He turned toward the lens, its prisms catching the grey afternoon light. \"It won't know when a man is standing on the rocks in the dark, waiting to see if anyone is out there. It won't write anything down.\"\n\nPriya was quiet. Then she asked if he would show her how the mechanism worked, and he did, patiently, the way he had once taught Ruth to change the wick when she had come out to visit for a single summer and stayed three days longer than planned.\n\nOn the final night before the switchover, Edwin climbed the stairs at dusk as he always had. He wound the weights. He trimmed the wick, polished the glass, and struck the match. The light rose slowly and swept out over the water, pale and steady, reaching across the dark toward the coast and whatever ships might still be sailing home.\n\nHe wrote the last entry in the eleventh logbook: *Wind northwest, moderate. Visibility good. Lamp lit at 4:52. All is well.* Then he sat on the iron step and watched the beam turn, one slow rotation after another, until the sky went from black to grey.\n\nIn the morning the boat came for him. He carried the logbooks down himself, one at a time, and stacked them carefully in the bow.\n\nBehind him, the light kept turning, alone now, and no one on the island was there to see it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":959,"cache_read_input_tokens":4215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":959,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu-1","agent_id":"tid-1","session_id":"sess-1","uuid":"uuid-46","timestamp":"2026-11-24T01:39:55.526Z","request_id":"apireq-2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write lighthouse keeper story"}
